{"step_index":38,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-08T20:51:39Z","input_tokens":3022,"cache_read_tokens":46368,"output_tokens":4493,"thinking":"The configuration file needs to be created, adhering to the constraint of zero in-code documentation. Examining the user-provided Firebase configuration is the next step.\n\n\nExamining the Firebase configuration, it seems straightforward to initialize the Firebase app using the provided API key, auth domain, and project ID. Necessary imports include the Firebase app and firestore modules to access the database functions. The plan now is to define default questions, using the 20 questions from the provided HTML file as a fallback, ensuring the database has initial data.\n\n\nThe switch statement questions reveal key concepts: the `default` case acts as a fallback when no other case matches; without `break` statements, execution \"falls through\" to subsequent cases; and case labels must be constant integer or character values. Evaluating `default`'s usage and `break` omission shows the fall-through behavior, confirming correct assessments.\n\n\nThe switch statement analysis continues, focusing on practical edge cases and syntax. A duplicate case value leads to a compile-time error. The `default` label's position within a switch statement is flexible, appearing anywhere. Understanding fall-through behavior with stacked labels is key to predicting outputs. The controlling expression in a C switch cannot be a float variable; integer types are required. Case labels must be constant expressions, such as integers or characters.\n\n\nThe evaluation of the `switch` statement continues, examining more complex scenarios and common pitfalls. Focus is on fall-through behavior, order of execution with and without breaks, and valid syntax. The `switch` statement's preference for single variable equality checks against constants is confirmed. Now reviewing nested switch structures.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_file","args":{"Content":"import { initializeApp } from \"https://www.gstatic.com/firebasejs/10.14.0/firebase-app.js\";\nimport { getFirestore, doc, getDoc, setDoc, addDoc, collection } from \"https://www.gstatic.com/firebasejs/10.14.0/firebase-firestore.js\";\n\nconst firebaseConfig = {\n  apiKey: \"AIzaSyBkqJ9WvtXfGJt19XK7SLK9v5cJwp4EVII\",\n  authDomain: \"sir-jasper-quiz.firebaseapp.com\",\n  projectId: \"sir-jasper-quiz\",\n  storageBucket: \"sir-jasper-quiz.firebasestorage.app\",\n  messagingSenderId: \"932129654171\",\n  appId: \"1:932129654171:web:793aa17dd1f130a62f4d3e\"\n};\n\nconst app = initializeApp(firebaseConfig);\nconst db = getFirestore(app);\n\nexport const defaultQuiz = {\n  title: \"C Quiz: Switch Case\",\n  description: \"20 items • 20 seconds per question • Do not switch tabs or windows.\",\n  settings: {\n    timePerQuestion: 20,\n    enableTimer: true,\n    enableTabViolation: true,\n    tabViolationPenalty: 1,\n    randomizeQuestions: true,\n    randomizeAnswers: true\n  },\n  questions: [\n    {\n      q: \"Which C statement selects one of many code blocks to run based on the value of an expression?\",\n      correct: \"switch\",\n      wrongs: [\"for\", \"while\", \"goto\"],\n      explanation: \"switch compares one expression against a list of constant case labels and runs the matching block.\"\n    },\n    {\n      q: \"Which type of expression can be used as the controlling expression of a switch in C?\",\n      correct: \"int or char\",\n      wrongs: [\"float\", \"double\", \"string (char*)\"],\n      explanation: \"switch works with integral types such as int and char, not floating-point values or strings.\"\n    },\n    {\n      q: \"Which keyword prevents execution from falling through to the next case?\",\n      correct: \"break\",\n      wrongs: [\"continue\", \"return 0\", \"exit\"],\n      explanation: \"break leaves the switch block immediately after the matching case finishes.\"\n    },\n    {\n      q: \"When does the default label run?\",\n      correct: \"When no case label matches\",\n      wrongs: [\"Always, before the cases\", \"Only when the first case fails\", \"Only if break is missing\"],\n      explanation: \"default is the fallback block used when no case matches the expression.\"\n    },\n    {\n      q: \"What is the output?\\nint x = 2;\\nswitch (x) {\\n  case 1: printf(\\\"A\\\");\\n  case 2: printf(\\\"B\\\");\\n  case 3: printf(\\\"C\\\");\\n}\",\n      correct: \"BC\",\n      wrongs: [\"B\", \"C\", \"ABC\"],\n      explanation: \"No break statements, so execution starts at case 2 and falls through to case 3.\"\n    },\n    {\n      q: \"What is the output?\\nint x = 3;\\nswitch (x) {\\n  case 1: printf(\\\"1\\\"); break;\\n  default: printf(\\\"D\\\");\\n}\",\n      correct: \"D\",\n      wrongs: [\"1\", \"1D\", \"Nothing is printed\"],\n      explanation: \"x is 3, no case matches, so default runs.\"\n    },\n    {\n      q: \"Is the default label required in a switch statement?\",\n      correct: \"No, it is optional\",\n      wrongs: [\"Yes, always\", \"Yes, but only with char\", \"Only if there is no break\"],\n      explanation: \"default is optional; without it, nothing runs when no case matches.\"\n    },\n    {\n      q: \"Which case label is valid in C?\",\n      correct: \"case 'A':\",\n      wrongs: [\"case x:  (x is a variable)\", \"case 1.5:\", \"case \\\"A\\\":\"],\n      explanation: \"Case labels must be integer or character constants. Variables, floats and strings are not allowed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      q: \"What is the output?\\nchar c = 'b';\\nswitch (c) {\\n  case 'a': printf(\\\"1\\\"); break;\\n  case 'b': printf(\\\"2\\\");\\n  case 'c': printf(\\\"3\\\"); break;\\n}\",\n      correct: \"23\",\n      wrongs: [\"2\", \"3\", \"123\"],\n      explanation: \"case 'b' matches and prints 2, has no break, then falls into case 'c' and prints 3 before break.\"\n    },\n    {\n      q: \"What happens if two case labels in the same switch have the same constant value?\",\n      correct: \"Compile-time error\",\n      wrongs: [\"The first one runs\", \"The last one runs\", \"Both run\"],\n      explanation: \"Duplicate case values are not allowed and the compiler reports an error.\"\n    },\n    {\n      q: \"Where can the default label be placed inside a switch?\",\n      correct: \"Anywhere inside the switch\",\n      wrongs: [\"Only at the end\", \"Only at the beginning\", \"Only after a break\"],\n      explanation: \"default may appear in any position; it is used only when no case matches.\"\n    },\n    {\n      q: \"What is the output when x = 1?\\nswitch (x) {\\n  case 1:\\n  case 2: printf(\\\"Low\\\"); break;\\n  case 3: printf(\\\"High\\\");\\n}\",\n      correct: \"Low\",\n      wrongs: [\"High\", \"LowHigh\", \"Nothing is printed\"],\n      explanation: \"Stacked labels share one block, so case 1 falls into case 2's code, which prints Low and breaks.\"\n    },\n    {\n      q: \"Which is NOT allowed as the controlling expression of a switch in C?\",\n      correct: \"A float variable, e.g. switch(f)\",\n      wrongs: [\"An int variable, e.g. switch(x)\", \"A char variable, e.g. switch(c)\", \"An expression, e.g. switch(x % 3)\"],\n      explanation: \"The controlling expression must be an integer type; float values are not allowed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      q: \"Which statement about case labels is true?\",\n      correct: \"They must be constant expressions\",\n      wrongs: [\"They can be any variable\", \"They can be float values\", \"They can be strings\"],\n      explanation: \"The compiler needs constant integer or character values to build the jump table.\"\n    },\n    {\n      q: \"What is the output?\\nint x = 5;\\nswitch (x % 2) {\\n  case 0: printf(\\\"Even\\\"); break;\\n  case 1: printf(\\\"Odd\\\"); break;\\n}\",\n      correct: \"Odd\",\n      wrongs: [\"Even\", \"EvenOdd\", \"Nothing is printed\"],\n      explanation: \"5 % 2 is 1, so case 1 runs.\"\n    },\n    {\n      q: \"What is the output?\\nint x = 1;\\nswitch (x) {\\n  default: printf(\\\"D\\\");\\n  case 1: printf(\\\"1\\\"); break;\\n  case 2: printf(\\\"2\\\");\\n}\",\n      correct: \"1\",\n      wrongs: [\"D1\", \"D\", \"12\"],\n      explanation: \"x matches case 1 directly, so default is skipped. Only 1 is printed, then break.\"\n    },\n    {\n      q: \"When is switch preferred over an if-else if chain?\",\n      correct: \"Comparing one variable against several constant values\",\n      wrongs: [\"Testing ranges like x > 10\", \"Comparing strings\", \"Evaluating floating-point conditions\"],\n      explanation: \"switch is designed for equality checks of one integral value against constants. It cannot test ranges.\"\n    },\n    {\n      q: \"What is the value printed?\\nint a = 1;\\nswitch (a) {\\n  case 1: a++;\\n  case 2: a += 2; break;\\n  case 3: a = 10;\\n}\\nprintf(\\\"%d\\\", a);\",\n      correct: \"4\",\n      wrongs: [\"3\", \"2\", \"10\"],\n      explanation: \"case 1 makes a = 2, falls into case 2 so a = 4, then break.\"\n    },\n    {\n      q: \"Which is the correct switch syntax?\",\n      correct: \"switch (x) { case 1: printf(\\\"One\\\"); break; }\",\n      wrongs: [\"switch x { case 1: printf(\\\"One\\\"); break; }\", \"switch (x) { case 1 printf(\\\"One\\\"); break; }\", \"switch (x) { case(1) = printf(\\\"One\\\"); }\"],\n      explanation: \"The expression goes in parentheses and each case label ends with a colon.\"\n    },\n    {\n      q: \"Can a switch statement be nested inside another switch statement in C?\",\n      correct: \"Yes\",\n      wrongs: [\"No\", \"Only inside default\", \"Only with goto\"],\n      explanation: \"Switch statements can be nested; each break only exits the innermost switch.\"\n    }\n  ]\n};\n\nexport async function getActiveQuiz(quizId = \"active\") {\n  try {\n    const snap = await getDoc(doc(db, \"quizzes\", quizId));\n    if (snap.exists()) {\n      return snap.data();\n    }\n  } catch (err) {}\n  return defaultQuiz;\n}\n\nexport async function saveActiveQuiz(quizData, quizId = \"active\") {\n  const ref = doc(db, \"quizzes\", quizId);\n  await setDoc(ref, {\n    ...quizData,\n    updatedAt: Date.now()\n  });\n  return true;\n}\n\nexport async function saveSubmission(resultData, quizId = \"active\") {\n  try {\n    const subCol = collection(db, \"quizzes\", quizId, \"submissions\");\n    await addDoc(subCol, {\n      ...resultData,\n      submittedAt: Date.now()\n    });\n  } catch (err) {}\n}\n\nexport { app, db };\n","Path":"C:\\Users\\CxAdmin\\Desktop\\Exploit\\firebase-config.js"}}]}
